---

Every harness runs the same checker, `hdr hook`, which reads one event as JSON on
standard input and answers in Claude Code's hook format. Claude Code and Codex
call it directly as a command hook. opencode and pi load code in-process instead,
so HDR installs a small generated plugin that converts their events, runs the
checker, and turns its answer into that harness's way of blocking.

# At a glance

| Harness | Installed as | Project location | Blocks tool calls | Failing Stop check |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Claude Code | Command hooks in settings | `.claude/settings.json` | yes | Agent is asked to continue once |
| Codex | Command hooks | `.codex/hooks.json` | yes, see status below | Agent is asked to continue once |
| opencode | Generated plugin | `.opencode/plugins/hdr.ts` | yes | Warning only |
| pi | Generated extension | `.pi/extensions/hdr.ts` | yes | One follow-up message, then control returns |

User-level installs go to `~/.claude/settings.json`, `$CODEX_HOME/hooks.json`,
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/opencode/plugins/hdr.ts` and
`$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/extensions/hdr.ts`, each falling back to the harness's
usual home directory.

# How file tools are recognised

The append-only guard needs to know when a tool call edits a file. Each
harness's native tools are mapped to the same two operations:

| Harness | Treated as an edit | Treated as a write |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Claude Code | `Edit`, `MultiEdit`, `NotebookEdit` | `Write` |
| Codex | `apply_patch` update, delete or move | `apply_patch` add |
| opencode | `edit`, `apply_patch` update, delete or move | `write`, `apply_patch` add |
| pi | `edit` | `write` |

Shell tools are not parsed. Changes made through a shell are caught afterwards
by the session baseline, as described in [Decision history]($link.page('history')).
`tool_denied` decisions use each harness's own tool names, so a rule for Claude's
`Bash` does not cover pi's `bash`.

# Claude Code

```sh
hdr install claude --project
```

The reference integration. Hooks are added to the settings file for
`SessionStart`, `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse` and `Stop`, keeping any existing
settings and a one-time `.hdr-backup` copy. HDR also reads the user, project and
local settings and flags `disableAllHooks` and a configured `bypassPermissions`
mode. A session running in bypass mode has every tool call denied.

# Codex

```sh
hdr install codex --project
```

Codex uses the same hook protocol and event names as Claude Code, so the
integration is the same command written to `.codex/hooks.json`. `apply_patch`
envelopes are parsed so each file they touch is checked.

># [Status: not yet verified live]($block.attrs('warning'))
>The installer and payload handling are covered by tests, but enforcement has not
>yet been confirmed in a real Codex session. Codex loads project hooks only when
>the project is trusted, and skips any non-managed hook until someone approves it
>in `/hooks`. Approval is tied to the hook's hash, so reinstalling HDR asks for
>approval again. `hdr check` can only see that the hooks are on disk, not whether
>Codex approved them.

Hosted tools such as web search do not trigger Codex hooks.

# opencode

```sh
hdr install opencode --project
```

HDR writes a plugin to `.opencode/plugins/hdr.ts`. It uses `tool.execute.before`
to block a call by throwing the HDR reason, `tool.execute.after` to append
feedback to the tool's output, and session events for start and stop.

- A failing Stop check only shows a warning toast: a plugin cannot make an idle
  session continue.
- At the time of writing, opencode does not run `tool.execute.before` for tool
  calls made by subagents ([opencode#5894](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/5894)).
  Those calls are only caught afterwards by the session baseline.

# pi

```sh
hdr install pi --project
```

HDR writes an extension to `.pi/extensions/hdr.ts`. It blocks through
`tool_call`, appends feedback to tool results through `tool_result`, gives the
agent the session-start context through `before_agent_start`, and checks Stop
through `agent_end`.

- pi loads project extensions only after you trust the project. Non-interactive
  runs need `--approve` or a saved trust decision.
- A failing Stop check sends one follow-up message asking for a correction, then
  returns control, mirroring Claude Code's behaviour.

# Plugins fail closed

The opencode and pi plugins deny the tool call if the checker cannot run at all,
for example if Python is missing or the hook times out. HDR counts a plugin as
installed only while it matches the generated file byte for byte, so a hand-edited
plugin is reported as missing. Reinstall rather than edit it.
